THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL AND UNDERLYING FACTS
-----------------------------------------

The claim of ownership above covers this project's own expression: the
written descriptions, the vector ink drawings generated by
assets/sketches.js, the stylised journal map in assets/app.js, the visual
design, and the code. It does not and cannot extend to the following.

1. FACTS ARE NOT OWNED.
   That a species is Critically Endangered, that a survey counted 244 birds,
   that an assessment was published in 2020 — these are statements of fact
   and are not subject to copyright. They are reproduced here as facts, with
   the body that established them named on every page where they appear and
   cited in full at /references.html. No descriptive text, photograph,
   illustration, or bulk database extract has been copied from any source in
   that bibliography.

   Use of IUCN Red List data is consistent with the IUCN Red List Terms of
   Use, which permit non-commercial use with attribution. The same principle
   of attribution has been applied to every other register cited, including
   NatureServe, the US Fish and Wildlife Service, NOAA Fisheries, COSEWIC,
   SANBI, ICMBio, DCCEEW, the New Zealand Department of Conservation, CITES
   and CMS.

2. ORGANISATION NAMES AND LINKS.
   The conservation organisations listed in data/organizations.json are named
   and linked for the reader's benefit. Their names, marks and websites remain
   their own property. Their inclusion is editorial and unpaid: this project
   holds no affiliate arrangement with any of them, takes no commission, and
   receives nothing if a reader gives.

3. HISTORICAL PLATES.
   Most species figures are natural-history plates whose copyright has expired
   — lithographs and engravings from works such as Brehm's Tierleben,
   Lydekker's handbooks and Gould's monographs, published in 1928 or earlier.
   These are in the public domain and are not claimed here. Each is credited
   under the figure and listed at /references.html#plate-list with its artist,
   source work, year of publication, licence and a link to the source file, so
   the claim can be checked.

   A faithful photographic reproduction of a two-dimensional public-domain
   artwork does not attract a new copyright in the United States (Bridgeman Art
   Library v. Corel Corp., 36 F. Supp. 2d 191), and Article 14 of Directive
   (EU) 2019/790 provides the same in the European Union. The scan is therefore
   as free as the engraving it reproduces. Provenance for every plate is
   re-checked weekly by scripts/verify.py.

   Where no plate exists — for species described after the plate era — the
   figure is an original drawing produced for this archive, and the caption
   says so.

4. FONTS.
   Fraunces, EB Garamond and Caveat are used under the SIL Open Font License
   and are served from Google Fonts. They are not covered by this licence.

5. FLAGS.
   The flags in assets/flags.js are drawn from compact geometric specifications
   written for this project. Flags of state are not copyrightable subject
   matter; regardless, none of this artwork is traced or copied from any
   existing rendering, and no flag image is bundled or hot-linked. Where a flag
   carries an intricate coat of arms, the device is drawn as a simplified
   emblem rather than reproduced, and the register says so.
